# Inequalities: When Answers Are Ranges, Not Points

> Equations pin a variable to exact values; inequalities describe
> whole ranges of them. Salary brackets, medicine doses, safety
> margins, and optimisation constraints are all inequalities — and
> solving them has one golden rule: negatives flip the sign.

## 1. The Language of Inequalities

| Symbol | Meaning | Example solution set |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $<$, $>$ | strictly less / greater | $x > 3$ |
| $\le$, $\ge$ | or equal to | $x \le 3$ |

A solution is a **range**, usually drawn on a number line or written
in interval notation: $x > 3$ is $(3, \infty)$; $x \le 3$ is
$(-\infty, 3]$.

[definition]
An **open interval** $(a, b)$ excludes its endpoints; a **closed
interval** $[a, b]$ includes them. $x \ge 2$ and $x < 5$ together
give $[2, 5)$ — closed at 2, open at 5.
[/definition]

## 2. The Golden Rule: Multiplying by a Negative

You may add or subtract anything from both sides freely. But multiply
or divide both sides by a **negative number** and the direction of the
inequality **reverses**:

$$3 < 5 \quad\text{yet}\quad -3 > -5$$

@math label="eq:flip-example"
-2x < 6 \;\Rightarrow\; x > -3 \quad (\text{divide by } -2,\ \text{flip})
@/math

[warning]
Forgetting the flip is the single most common inequality error — and
it silently produces a wrong half-line. Check by substitution: with
$x = 0 > -3$, indeed $-2(0) = 0 < 6$. ✓
[/warning]

## 3. Linear Inequalities

**Example.** Solve $3x - 4 \le 11$:

@math label="eq:linear-example"
3x \le 15 \;\Rightarrow\; x \le 5 \;\Rightarrow\; (-\infty,\ 5]
@/math

**Double inequalities.** Solve $-4 \le 2x + 1 < 7$ by operating on
all three parts at once:

@math label="eq:double-example"
-5 \le 2x < 6 \;\Rightarrow\; -2.5 \le x < 3 \;\Rightarrow\; [-2.5,\ 3)
@/math

## 4. Applications: Brackets, Budgets, and Doses

- **Tax brackets:** income up to ₹2.5 lakh at 0%, ₹2.5–5 lakh at 5%,
  and so on — each bracket is a compound inequality.
- **Budgeting:** a taxi charges ₹50 plus ₹12/km. How many kilometres
  fit a ₹200 budget?

@math label="eq:taxi-example"
50 + 12k \le 200 \;\Rightarrow\; 12k \le 150 \;\Rightarrow\; k \le 12.5
@/math

At most 12 full kilometres.

## 5. Quadratic Inequalities

Graph thinking beats algebra here. Solve $x^2 - x - 6 > 0$:

1. Find roots: $(x-3)(x+2) = 0$ gives $x = -2, 3$.
2. The parabola opens upward, so it is **above** zero outside the
   roots and below between them.
3. Answer: $x < -2$ or $x > 3$.

@chart spline title="Sign of y = x² − x − 6 across the number line"
labels:
−3
−2
0
3
4

series:
  - name: "y"
    data: 6 0 -6 0 6
@/chart

[tip]
The "outside-between" rule: for a positive quadratic ($x^2$ positive),
"$> 0$" holds outside the roots, "$< 0$" between them. Flip this for
a negative quadratic. When the quadratic has no real roots, its sign
never changes — test one point and you know it everywhere.
[/tip]

## 6. Absolute Value Inequalities

Recall $|x|$ is the distance of $x$ from 0. Distance language turns
these into range problems:

@math label="eq:abs-less"
|x| < a \;\Longleftrightarrow\; -a < x < a \qquad (a > 0)
@/math

@math label="eq:abs-greater"
|x| > a \;\Longleftrightarrow\; x < -a \ \text{ or } \ x > a
@/math

**Example.** Solve $|2x - 1| \le 7$:

@math label="eq:abs-example"
-7 \le 2x - 1 \le 7 \;\Rightarrow\; -6 \le 2x \le 8 \;\Rightarrow\; -3 \le x \le 4
@/math

**Real use.** A machine fills 500 mL bottles with error at most
10 mL: $|V - 500| \le 10$ means every bottle holds between 490 and
510 mL.

## 7. Systems and the Number Line

For several conditions at once, mark each on a number line and keep
the overlap ("and") or the union ("or").

**Example.** Find $x$ such that $x + 2 > 3$ **and** $2x \le 10$:

- $x > 1$ and $x \le 5$

- Answer: $(1, 5]$

## 8. A Taste of Optimisation

Businesses live inside inequalities: production capacity, budget,
demand. Suppose a bakery makes ₹40 profit per cake and ₹25 per plate
of cookies, with flour for at most 30 cakes or 60 plates:

@math label="eq:optimisation"
40c + 25p \to \text{max}, \quad \text{subject to } 2c + p \le 60,\ c, p \ge 0
@/math

This is **linear programming**: the best answer hides at a corner of
the feasible region — a triangle whose sides are the inequalities.
Maximising subject to constraints scales up to airline scheduling
and supply chains.

[summary]
Inequalities describe ranges, written in interval notation. Golden
rule: multiplying or dividing by a negative flips the sign. Linear
inequalities solve like equations; double inequalities operate on
all parts together. For quadratics, use the roots and the "outside /
between" sign pattern. Absolute values translate into distance
ranges — $|x| < a$ gives a strip, $|x| > a$ gives two tails. Combine
multiple conditions on a number line, and you hold the basic tool of
optimisation: feasible regions carved out by inequalities.
[/summary]
